>=20
> I got the same problem on an x86-PC and after looking at the stack, t=
he=20
> problem comes from firmware_map_add_early() in drivers/firmware/memma=
p.c
>=20
> The backtrace is the following:
>=20
> kzalloc()
> verify_dynamic_kobject_allocation()
> kobject_init()
> firmware_map_add_entry()
> firmware_map_add_early()
> e820_reserve_resources()
> setup_arch() (in x86)
>=20
> The problem is that verify_dynamic_kobject_allocation() calls kzalloc=
=20
> assuming that kmem_cache_init() has already been called. But it's not=
=20
> the case and it's too soon to call the kmalloc/kzalloc functions.
>=20
> I don't know what is the real problem: the fact that kobject_init is=20
> called too soon or verify_dynamic_kobject_allocation() which calls=20
> kzalloc without making any assumption about its current context.
>=20
> So here is just a patch to temporarily disable=20
> verify_dynamic_kobject_allocation() This function just checks the san=
ity=20
> of the code.

Confirmed that this fixes the boot problem on my machine, too.
(It explains why this happens only on x86-32...)

Added Bernhard to Cc.  Maybe we should defer firmware_map_add*()?


thanks,

Takashi


